WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.658 --> 00:00:04.700
OLIVER OGG: Good morning.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:09.020
Hello, everyone, and welcome
to the Apigee online meetup.

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:14.030
My name is Oliver and I
will be your host today.

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:15.530
Because of the
current situation,

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:19.640
again, we are broadcasting from
our respective homes instead

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.020
of the London offices.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:24.230
And we're very excited
to have you join us today

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:25.550
for our conversation.

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:33.880
Today marks our 10th
Apigee online meetup, back

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:35.680
by popular demand.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:39.550
It is our goal to be
as interactive and as

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:42.440
educational as possible.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:45.760
The region we broadcast
to is diverse,

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.310
so I look forward to your
perspectives, your comments

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:52.020
and questions, throughout
today's session.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.280
We've got a lot to
get through today.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.300
So after this
introduction, I'm going

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:05.870
to give a short introduction to
running an API-first platform

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:07.070
business.

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:08.960
And then we'll hear
from Simon, who

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.510
will talk about driving
commercial impact

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:14.750
with a true platform strategy.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:16.580
We'll then, at
about halfway point,

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:21.860
have an opportunity to
take questions from you.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:24.350
We'll then roll into our
fireside chat between Simon

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.870
and myself, and then
towards the end,

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:30.320
have another opportunity
to answer questions

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.450
from yourselves.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.720
So there's lots of
opportunity to ask questions

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:37.850
throughout this session.

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:41.660
Please don't just wait
towards these windows

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:45.600
or towards the end of the
meetup to ask those questions.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.930
Please, as we go forward,
if something comes to mind,

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:53.330
please do ask them in
the ON24 tool here.

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:56.960
Today speaker-- I'm very pleased
to say that, we're joined today

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.150
by Simon Torrance.

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:03.200
Simon is the creator of
"The New Growth Playbook,"

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.260
co-founder of the
platform Economy Summit,

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.870
and co-author of "FightBack--

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:13.400
How to Win in a Digital Economy
with Platforms, Ventures,

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.440
and Entrepreneurs."

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:18.890
So without further ado,
get yourself comfortable.

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:20.860
Let's get started.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:24.410
So to warm things up and get
everybody on the same page,

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:28.940
let's talk about API-first
platform businesses--

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:32.900
executing with that
platform mindset.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:35.150
For the digital native
platform businesses,

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:38.300
like Google, APIs
are in our DNA.

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:42.290
It is how we share data
within the business

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:44.780
and outside the business.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:49.580
There are substantial commercial
and transformative effects

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:52.040
if you execute well.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:56.210
I'll touch on some key
examples of business outcomes

00:02:56.210 --> 00:03:01.640
our customers have realized
as a result of executing well.

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:04.910
Now, one of the
challenges around APIs

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:08.330
is the fact that they've been
around for such a long time.

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:12.710
People's perspective of them is
that they're about technology

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:14.750
and integration and access--

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:19.310
this left hand side
of this sliding scale.

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:21.710
Apigee and myself
spent a lot of time

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:25.340
helping customers really think
about this right hand side,

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:28.100
where these
substantial effects can

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:31.850
be realized because now a
business can efficiently

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:35.810
participate in vibrant
digital ecosystems--

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:40.370
experiment, launch new
business models, new ventures

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:45.200
and monetize in new
and different ways.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.570
The organizations
that do this well

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:50.540
are where APIs aren't
just in the periphery.

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:53.420
APIs are now central to
everything that they do,

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:55.490
this API-first culture.

00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:58.850
So APIs are built by
default. But APIs are just

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:01.970
how data is shared
through the business

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:04.380
and in and out of that business.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:07.130
Many of you will be familiar
with that Jeff Bezos mandate

00:04:07.130 --> 00:04:10.940
in 2002, no less.

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:15.500
Now that really launched that
API-first culture in Amazon.

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:17.420
And most industry
observers would

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:20.540
say that that was
key to how Amazon

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:25.760
has shown extraordinary growth
and adaptability and resilience

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:29.420
as that business has evolved.

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:32.720
These are sort of examples
where business realizes

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:37.550
network effects of growth, new
revenue streams, resilience

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:39.530
and adaptability.

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:42.800
There are other, larger
financial incentives

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.710
to execute a platform strategy.

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:49.280
McKinsey believes that by 2025--

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.680
so not that far
away-- $60 trillion

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:56.680
is going to flow through
digital platforms.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:00.520
Benzell, Lagarda, and Van
Alstyne in that academic paper

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:04.240
called "The Impact of
APIs in Firm Performance"

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:07.130
talk about a number
of benefits--

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:09.460
financial benefits--
to an organization.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:11.650
And the call out
there is that they

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:15.130
believe that you can achieve
a 12% increase in market

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:18.870
capitalization if
you execute well.

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:21.570
Now, there are more tangible,
more immediate business

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:24.530
outcomes, which I'll
touch on in a moment.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:30.810
So an API Platform and
strategically placing them

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:33.210
in the heart of your
business enables

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:37.680
you to control and
enable the flow of data

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.920
through supply and demand.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:42.580
Let's go through supply here.

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:44.460
So it may well be
that you already today

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:47.790
have a rich catalog of
products and services,

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:49.590
perhaps from lots
of different parts

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:54.510
of the business, if you're
a large group company.

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:57.510
For many of our customers, it
is about aggregating supply

00:05:57.510 --> 00:05:59.910
from partners and startups.

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:03.120
So for example, leveraging
another company's

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:07.740
products and services,
or indeed, content.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:10.560
Many of our customers are
trying to interact much more

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:13.080
efficiently with
other industries--

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:16.650
telcos, banks, logistics,
classic examples

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:22.110
of where a business now needs to
be very efficiently interacting

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:24.780
with other industries.

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:27.420
And of course, things
at the bottom there,

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:31.650
for particularly like technology
companies, smart cities,

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:36.540
telcos, there's often a huge
fleet of devices and sensors

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:37.260
out there.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:39.660
And you need to
aggregate that data

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:42.730
and flow it into the business.

00:06:42.730 --> 00:06:46.010
And then demand--
so again, partners.

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:49.960
It may well be that a channel
for your products and services

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:53.290
will be strategic partners.

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:56.350
It might be the long
tail through third party

00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:59.710
applications and developers.

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:02.080
Social channels, of
which there's always

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:05.110
a new one popping up
every couple of years.

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:10.210
Of course, omnichannel
and devices of all sorts.

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:14.560
And of course, mobile channels.

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:19.270
So by strategically placing
that API platform in the middle,

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:22.060
it enables you to insert
that control plane

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:26.920
as data flows in and out and
through the organization.

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:30.010
It enables you to
govern that data,

00:07:30.010 --> 00:07:34.240
protect it, but also unlock
it where appropriate.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:35.290
Package it.

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:36.610
Productize it.

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:38.530
And monetize it.

00:07:38.530 --> 00:07:42.160
Now, monetization is
a key data strategy.

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:48.320
And it's often routed around
some sort of partner strategy.

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:51.110
This enables you now to create
these highly unusual assets,

00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:52.220
these APIs.

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:56.810
And it enables you to
decouple old and new worlds,

00:07:56.810 --> 00:08:00.830
or different parts of
that demand-supply balance

00:08:00.830 --> 00:08:04.880
that want to operate
at different speeds.

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:07.910
Simon is going to talk about
this model of supply and demand

00:08:07.910 --> 00:08:08.690
a little bit more.

00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:11.450
He's also going to talk
about innovation models

00:08:11.450 --> 00:08:14.370
that you can drive
into corporate.

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:18.290
APIs really help an
organization and unlock

00:08:18.290 --> 00:08:21.200
that innovation
whilst also leveraging

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:24.380
those efficiencies of
scale, those assets

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:28.000
that an enterprise has.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:29.500
Data is fundamental here.

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:33.470
So APIs and this API layer gives
you this unique opportunity

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:36.470
in capturing data consistently--

00:08:36.470 --> 00:08:38.960
large, large volumes of data.

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:42.610
So it is also important that
you're capturing that data,

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:45.010
you're not scared of
the volumes of data,

00:08:45.010 --> 00:08:47.890
that you are
democratizing that data,

00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:52.340
and you are giving the different
teams within your business

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:54.160
that data so they
can drive insight

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:57.790
into their different
product areas.

00:08:57.790 --> 00:09:00.985
This slide emphasizes
now this future state

00:09:00.985 --> 00:09:04.540
for organizations that they
are becoming orchestrators,

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:08.380
facilitators between
supply and demand,

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:13.150
facilitating highly
efficient transactions.

00:09:13.150 --> 00:09:17.020
I talked about some
tangible commercial effects

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:18.850
that some of our businesses--

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:22.420
some of our customers
have realized.

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:26.500
To partners is a key KPI
for many of our customers,

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:31.190
often coming from a world where
onboarding used to take years,

00:09:31.190 --> 00:09:32.810
if not several months.

00:09:32.810 --> 00:09:36.430
So this is a KPI
from a telco where

00:09:36.430 --> 00:09:39.910
they could increase the time--

00:09:39.910 --> 00:09:43.300
they could reduce, sorry, the
time to on board from two years

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:45.480
to two weeks.

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:48.450
Now, that is an
important KPI for anybody

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:53.820
on a journey of a
partner eco-system.

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:56.580
Scale-- a technology
company we work with

00:09:56.580 --> 00:10:00.390
runs a platform at 10x the scale
of other projects with 1/10

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:04.260
the workforce because that
platform there gives you

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:09.600
a unique opportunity to
facilitate these large groups

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:14.550
of supply and demand.

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:17.840
Optionality-- so it's not
just about speed here.

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:20.500
It's also the cost in
which to do something.

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:22.200
So this comes from
a retailer where

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:24.450
they could increase the
frequency of different channel

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:27.670
deployments by 30 times.

00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:30.430
That meant, for example,
that they could also

00:10:30.430 --> 00:10:35.230
quickly and cheaply launch
new products, new offerings,

00:10:35.230 --> 00:10:37.180
digital offerings
to their customers

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:40.240
to validate as to
whether they were

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:43.090
going to be impactful
products in their own right.

00:10:43.090 --> 00:10:47.050
New call center applications,
new mobile applications--

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:51.490
these were complex apps
built within weeks.

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:54.190
And lastly, products
and services--

00:10:54.190 --> 00:10:57.700
a retailer here increased their
product count 10x from 50,000

00:10:57.700 --> 00:11:02.050
to 1.5 million SKUs,
product variants.

00:11:02.050 --> 00:11:04.450
And they did that
within the first year

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:08.440
of launching their marketplace,
the marketplace being

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:11.940
a type of platform strategy.

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:15.060
So these are kind of
good examples of KPIs

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:17.130
that excite the business
community today.

00:11:20.070 --> 00:11:23.490
I want to call out two customers
that are really demonstrating

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:25.260
this phenomenally well.

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:28.260
In the world of retail,
Magalu, Magazine Louiza,

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:29.850
based in Brazil.

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:31.590
And in the world of
financial services,

00:11:31.590 --> 00:11:35.790
Arity, which spun
out of Allstate.

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:38.250
They also showed two
very different examples

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:41.220
of driving innovation within
your business, of which Simon

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:43.050
will go into in more detail.

00:11:43.050 --> 00:11:46.020
Magalu reinvented
themselves, moving

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:49.290
from a traditional
world of transformation

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:51.360
where you're a
physical retailer.

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:53.580
With omnichannel, they
now see themselves

00:11:53.580 --> 00:11:57.690
as that digital platform
with a physical presence.

00:11:57.690 --> 00:12:01.320
And Allstate that had a
connected car program running

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:05.670
for many years recognized that
they should spin out Arity

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:10.820
so that it could execute
autonomously from the parent.

00:12:10.820 --> 00:12:14.900
Simon will touch on those
two types of innovations.

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:17.210
So with Magalu,
this is an example

00:12:17.210 --> 00:12:20.090
of a customer that has realized
those market capitalization

00:12:20.090 --> 00:12:23.240
increases if you look
at their stock price

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:25.980
over the last few years.

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:28.040
This is a business
really focused

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:29.930
on this and that
partner ecosystem.

00:12:29.930 --> 00:12:32.300
They used to sell tens
of thousands of SKUs.

00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:35.930
They now sell 13
million products.

00:12:35.930 --> 00:12:39.110
They have 15,000 sellers
on their platform,

00:12:39.110 --> 00:12:44.420
complex partners on board
within only two weeks.

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:47.810
Apigee helps you onboard
those partners efficiently.

00:12:47.810 --> 00:12:55.130
And since the last four years,
they've seen between 60% to 76%

00:12:55.130 --> 00:12:59.210
year on year growth
in e-commerce.

00:12:59.210 --> 00:13:03.920
With Arity, now, that connected
car program within Allstate

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:07.280
had a vast volume of data.

00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:11.990
But they recognized that there
were some industry challenges

00:13:11.990 --> 00:13:14.690
that they wanted to solve
by unlocking this data

00:13:14.690 --> 00:13:18.430
and monetizing their data.

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:21.970
So they wanted to
better understand

00:13:21.970 --> 00:13:25.990
risk, and more precisely,
price insurance.

00:13:25.990 --> 00:13:28.330
That means they need
the best insights, which

00:13:28.330 --> 00:13:30.610
means they need the most data.

00:13:30.610 --> 00:13:34.540
So by not just using
the data from the parent

00:13:34.540 --> 00:13:38.920
but opening up their APIs to
all sorts of participants,

00:13:38.920 --> 00:13:44.110
it means that they can Hoover up
much more data, thereby driving

00:13:44.110 --> 00:13:46.300
much more insights
through their models,

00:13:46.300 --> 00:13:49.780
and thereby making a much more
compelling service of which

00:13:49.780 --> 00:13:50.890
they monetize.

00:13:50.890 --> 00:13:53.830
They sit on over 85 years
worth of claims frequency

00:13:53.830 --> 00:13:56.920
and severity data, 10
years of experience

00:13:56.920 --> 00:14:00.370
capturing telematics data,
these sensors in things

00:14:00.370 --> 00:14:05.680
like phones and cars, 35 billion
miles of driving data built

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:07.322
into these models.

00:14:07.322 --> 00:14:09.280
They partner with mapping
companies and weather

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:10.660
companies.

00:14:10.660 --> 00:14:12.910
Customers include financial
services customers,

00:14:12.910 --> 00:14:16.720
transportation, ridesharing
companies, automakers,

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:18.810
and government.

00:14:18.810 --> 00:14:22.520
So this is a great example of a
virtuous cycle of data flowing

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:23.970
in and out of the business.

00:14:23.970 --> 00:14:25.970
They've even signed
up a competitor

00:14:25.970 --> 00:14:29.023
to their parent, Allstate,
because they just

00:14:29.023 --> 00:14:31.190
need more data, which just
makes them more valuable.

00:14:34.730 --> 00:14:35.230
Right.

00:14:35.230 --> 00:14:35.730
Thank you.

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:40.450
And now I'd like to
hand over to Simon.

00:14:40.450 --> 00:14:42.400
SIMON TORRANCE: Thanks
very much, Olly.

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:44.240
Very good to be here.

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:47.020
So I'm going to
talk about really

00:14:47.020 --> 00:14:49.000
what this picture shows here.

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:52.180
And you recognize that symbol.

00:14:52.180 --> 00:14:56.140
How can we, as
incumbent businesses,

00:14:56.140 --> 00:15:00.190
release the inner
digital Superman

00:15:00.190 --> 00:15:02.770
so we can create new
growth and value?

00:15:02.770 --> 00:15:05.530
And we're sort of trapped
within the body of Clark Kent

00:15:05.530 --> 00:15:08.110
at the moment in terms
of the ways that we think

00:15:08.110 --> 00:15:11.248
and the ways that we act and
our existing business model.

00:15:11.248 --> 00:15:13.540
And what I'm going to try
and show you over the next 20

00:15:13.540 --> 00:15:17.470
minutes is how we can apply
platform thinking and platform

00:15:17.470 --> 00:15:22.330
strategy to create a
significant commercial impact

00:15:22.330 --> 00:15:26.980
and create a new way to
grow in the digital economy.

00:15:26.980 --> 00:15:27.882
A bit of background--

00:15:27.882 --> 00:15:29.090
Olly's already introduced me.

00:15:29.090 --> 00:15:30.903
I won't go through
this in detail.

00:15:30.903 --> 00:15:32.320
I've written a
book on this topic.

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:36.520
I work with the World Economic
Forum on their accelerating

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:38.140
digital transformation program.

00:15:38.140 --> 00:15:42.280
And that is, again, about how
to help leaders and regulators

00:15:42.280 --> 00:15:45.460
and government think differently
about the types of business

00:15:45.460 --> 00:15:47.620
models that are going
to be most appropriate

00:15:47.620 --> 00:15:49.190
in the digital world.

00:15:49.190 --> 00:15:51.160
And I work with
execution partners

00:15:51.160 --> 00:15:55.360
like Rainmaking, a big corporate
innovation company and venture

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:57.790
building company, to help
corporates move forward

00:15:57.790 --> 00:16:00.400
with these principles.

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:04.930
And my experience is across
many sectors and geographies.

00:16:04.930 --> 00:16:10.690
And I'll touch on examples
from these during the talk.

00:16:10.690 --> 00:16:15.370
So the problem is summed up
by these three statistics

00:16:15.370 --> 00:16:17.260
you can see here.

00:16:17.260 --> 00:16:21.340
And my contention is that
digital transformation

00:16:21.340 --> 00:16:26.280
wasn't making a big
impact before COVID.

00:16:26.280 --> 00:16:31.300
COVID increases the importance
that we think differently

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:33.820
about how we operate
in a digital world.

00:16:33.820 --> 00:16:35.740
But you can see these
three statistics here

00:16:35.740 --> 00:16:42.700
from the surveys of C level
executives about 18 months ago.

00:16:42.700 --> 00:16:45.460
They were worried that their
existing business models were

00:16:45.460 --> 00:16:50.470
becoming increasingly unviable
as their [INAUDIBLE] digitizes

00:16:50.470 --> 00:16:53.620
because that increases
competition, opens up

00:16:53.620 --> 00:16:55.960
transparency about prices.

00:16:55.960 --> 00:16:59.210
And as they try and
compete with their--

00:16:59.210 --> 00:17:04.359
or keep up with consumer demands
and compete with other players,

00:17:04.359 --> 00:17:05.980
it increases costs.

00:17:05.980 --> 00:17:11.550
And in fact, 85% of
digital investments

00:17:11.550 --> 00:17:14.579
deliver zero or negative
economic value add.

00:17:14.579 --> 00:17:18.339
That's the profits
after cost of capital.

00:17:18.339 --> 00:17:21.270
And as a result of
this, even before COVID,

00:17:21.270 --> 00:17:24.450
executives were not particularly
happy with the innovation

00:17:24.450 --> 00:17:27.200
efforts of their organization.

00:17:27.200 --> 00:17:29.520
And now we're under
even more pressure.

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:33.690
The imperative to really
think differently and apply

00:17:33.690 --> 00:17:38.110
new business models
is just even bigger.

00:17:38.110 --> 00:17:40.620
And if you are an investor--

00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:42.390
I'm sure all of you
are and you have

00:17:42.390 --> 00:17:44.670
your own private investments--

00:17:44.670 --> 00:17:46.890
working [INAUDIBLE]
companies are also investors.

00:17:46.890 --> 00:17:48.800
Where do they place their bets?

00:17:48.800 --> 00:17:51.480
What type of business
models would you

00:17:51.480 --> 00:17:54.000
invest in compared to others?

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:57.780
And here's an example of
the returns of businesses

00:17:57.780 --> 00:18:00.420
that operate a platform
business model at the top

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:05.340
there compared to, I guess,
more traditional tech companies

00:18:05.340 --> 00:18:09.090
in the NASDAQ, the Dow
Jones, and, if you're

00:18:09.090 --> 00:18:12.000
an incumbent organization
for a traditional industry,

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:17.827
you tend to be represented
by the bottom figure there.

00:18:17.827 --> 00:18:20.160
So if you're thinking about
where you invest your money,

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:23.820
either as private
investors or as companies,

00:18:23.820 --> 00:18:26.280
maybe there are new
types of business models

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:29.730
that you should be investing
in which have more resilience

00:18:29.730 --> 00:18:31.650
and potential to grow.

00:18:31.650 --> 00:18:34.270
And my contention is that
there's our platform business

00:18:34.270 --> 00:18:34.770
model.

00:18:34.770 --> 00:18:38.070
And every company needs
to fully embrace them

00:18:38.070 --> 00:18:41.680
if they want to stay competitive
in a digital economy.

00:18:41.680 --> 00:18:43.180
And then it begs
the question, well,

00:18:43.180 --> 00:18:45.540
what do we mean by a
platform business model?

00:18:45.540 --> 00:18:47.903
And this is a very
simple description of it.

00:18:47.903 --> 00:18:49.680
It's a bit similar
to the one that Olly

00:18:49.680 --> 00:18:53.280
showed showing that platform
businesses essentially

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:57.900
act as intermediaries between
people who've got solutions

00:18:57.900 --> 00:18:59.550
and people who want solutions.

00:18:59.550 --> 00:19:01.620
And if you're an
incumbent corporation,

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:04.430
you provide some
of those solutions.

00:19:04.430 --> 00:19:06.900
But there are many other people
who could be complementary

00:19:06.900 --> 00:19:11.910
for you to create solutions
that serve your customers.

00:19:11.910 --> 00:19:14.970
And the aims of a
platform business

00:19:14.970 --> 00:19:17.610
are to facilitate high
value transactions,

00:19:17.610 --> 00:19:22.520
not just sell your own products,
and to create network effects

00:19:22.520 --> 00:19:24.800
that mean that your business
is more valuable the more

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:26.460
people who use it.

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:32.110
That's why eBay and Uber
and Airbnb are so powerful.

00:19:32.110 --> 00:19:34.760
They facilitate interactions.

00:19:34.760 --> 00:19:37.520
And the more people who use it--
the more suppliers, the more

00:19:37.520 --> 00:19:40.190
consumers-- the more
valuable it becomes.

00:19:40.190 --> 00:19:42.385
And the key requirements
are that you

00:19:42.385 --> 00:19:44.390
create an infrastructure,
like Olly said,

00:19:44.390 --> 00:19:46.670
enabled by APIs is
really critical.

00:19:46.670 --> 00:19:50.850
But anybody can plug
into your business.

00:19:50.850 --> 00:19:56.450
Now, how that translates
if we go forward--

00:19:56.450 --> 00:19:57.340
oh, yes.

00:19:57.340 --> 00:20:01.010
I've a slight technical problem.

00:20:01.010 --> 00:20:06.517
I hope you can see a picture of
a tweet by the CEO of Twitter.

00:20:06.517 --> 00:20:07.850
Yes, hopefully you can see that.

00:20:07.850 --> 00:20:11.260
Olly, can people see that?

00:20:11.260 --> 00:20:12.220
I hope so.

00:20:12.220 --> 00:20:13.810
OLIVER OGG: I'm probably
not the best person to ask,

00:20:13.810 --> 00:20:15.440
because I've been having
problems, as well.

00:20:15.440 --> 00:20:16.523
But yes, I can see it now.

00:20:16.523 --> 00:20:17.420
Yeah.

00:20:17.420 --> 00:20:18.760
SIMON TORRANCE: OK.

00:20:18.760 --> 00:20:19.330
Good.

00:20:19.330 --> 00:20:22.030
So the key essence of
a platform business

00:20:22.030 --> 00:20:26.080
is summed up by this tweet
from the CEO of Airbnb

00:20:26.080 --> 00:20:27.340
who said here, you can see it.

00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:30.010
"Marriott wants to add
30,000 rooms this year.

00:20:30.010 --> 00:20:32.320
We can do that in
the next two weeks."

00:20:32.320 --> 00:20:35.950
And it sums up the essential
power of a platform business

00:20:35.950 --> 00:20:40.930
model where you are leveraging
the assets of other people.

00:20:40.930 --> 00:20:44.740
You don't have to build
hotels to be able to provide

00:20:44.740 --> 00:20:47.560
a place for people to stay.

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:50.340
And for an incumbent
organization,

00:20:50.340 --> 00:20:52.090
there are many options
for where you could

00:20:52.090 --> 00:20:54.970
play in a platform economy.

00:20:54.970 --> 00:20:59.290
You could create your
own platform business.

00:20:59.290 --> 00:21:04.120
You could supply into
platform businesses.

00:21:04.120 --> 00:21:07.750
You could use platforms to
supply you with services.

00:21:07.750 --> 00:21:11.710
Or you could create
enabling capabilities

00:21:11.710 --> 00:21:15.550
that you make available
into platforms

00:21:15.550 --> 00:21:17.270
to help them run better.

00:21:17.270 --> 00:21:20.920
And as Olly said, 30% of
global economic activity

00:21:20.920 --> 00:21:25.150
is going to be mediated by
platforms in five years' time.

00:21:25.150 --> 00:21:27.940
That creates an enormous
market for people

00:21:27.940 --> 00:21:31.240
who've got-- who can create
enabling solutions that make

00:21:31.240 --> 00:21:33.010
those platforms run better.

00:21:33.010 --> 00:21:37.350
And financial services is
a golden example of this

00:21:37.350 --> 00:21:42.110
to provide financial services
to support platform businesses.

00:21:42.110 --> 00:21:47.800
So making this a bit more
tangible, what I've identified

00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:51.970
is six main types of platform
plays for corporate businesses

00:21:51.970 --> 00:21:54.982
and four types of
executional approaches.

00:21:54.982 --> 00:21:56.440
This is really
critical, because it

00:21:56.440 --> 00:22:00.010
helps to translate what can
feel like a very technical topic

00:22:00.010 --> 00:22:03.400
to senior commercial execs
into something more tangible.

00:22:03.400 --> 00:22:04.900
And I'll just
describe these quickly

00:22:04.900 --> 00:22:06.800
and show you some examples.

00:22:06.800 --> 00:22:09.190
So at the very,
very end there, you

00:22:09.190 --> 00:22:11.230
could reinvent your
company entirely

00:22:11.230 --> 00:22:14.410
and say we're going to become a
platform business, like Amazon

00:22:14.410 --> 00:22:18.240
did and Apple did 20 years ago.

00:22:18.240 --> 00:22:21.870
Or you can start more simply
on the bottom left here

00:22:21.870 --> 00:22:25.570
and say, well, we're trying
to create digital solutions.

00:22:25.570 --> 00:22:28.470
We don't have enough
capabilities and resources

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:29.970
in-house to do that.

00:22:29.970 --> 00:22:33.540
How could we tap into the
thousands, if not hundreds

00:22:33.540 --> 00:22:37.620
of thousands, of experts who
are out there in the world,

00:22:37.620 --> 00:22:41.370
freelance experts, who we
could use to help us create

00:22:41.370 --> 00:22:43.170
new digital solutions?

00:22:43.170 --> 00:22:45.180
And that would be a
very legitimate way

00:22:45.180 --> 00:22:47.370
of augmenting your
own capabilities

00:22:47.370 --> 00:22:48.930
with third parties.

00:22:48.930 --> 00:22:50.910
And some great example
I'm going to show you

00:22:50.910 --> 00:22:52.960
in a minute of a
company who's done that.

00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:54.950
You can create your
own digital solutions.

00:22:54.950 --> 00:22:56.700
And those would be
software-based products

00:22:56.700 --> 00:22:58.080
and services.

00:22:58.080 --> 00:23:02.010
And most unicorns
start off doing that.

00:23:02.010 --> 00:23:05.130
And then they evolve into
platform businesses over time.

00:23:05.130 --> 00:23:08.370
Why can't we, as incumbent
businesses, create our own

00:23:08.370 --> 00:23:09.630
unicorn businesses?

00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:10.380
There's no reason.

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:11.568
We have any assets there.

00:23:11.568 --> 00:23:13.110
There are a few
things missing, which

00:23:13.110 --> 00:23:15.150
I'll describe in a second.

00:23:15.150 --> 00:23:17.520
And of course, you can embed
your products and services

00:23:17.520 --> 00:23:20.340
or your digital solutions
into other people's platforms.

00:23:20.340 --> 00:23:21.990
You can create
online marketplaces,

00:23:21.990 --> 00:23:25.560
like we heard with
Magalu in South America.

00:23:25.560 --> 00:23:27.660
You can also
orchestrate completely

00:23:27.660 --> 00:23:32.010
new ecosystems that are
emerging at the boundaries

00:23:32.010 --> 00:23:33.990
between sectors.

00:23:33.990 --> 00:23:36.450
As these boundaries
are blurring,

00:23:36.450 --> 00:23:38.160
there are wonderful
opportunities

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:39.780
to create new
ecosystems that you can

00:23:39.780 --> 00:23:42.510
orchestrate with a platform.

00:23:42.510 --> 00:23:45.090
And in terms of the
way of executing,

00:23:45.090 --> 00:23:47.033
the problem that many
traditional companies

00:23:47.033 --> 00:23:49.200
have is they think we need
to build this internally.

00:23:49.200 --> 00:23:50.430
It's a big IT project.

00:23:50.430 --> 00:23:51.940
It's going to take five years.

00:23:51.940 --> 00:23:53.790
And that's the wrong
approach entirely.

00:23:53.790 --> 00:23:55.530
Occasionally you
can do something

00:23:55.530 --> 00:23:59.590
like that where you have a
particular power to do so.

00:23:59.590 --> 00:24:01.140
But there are other
approaches that

00:24:01.140 --> 00:24:04.920
are complementary to that which
need to be part of the armory,

00:24:04.920 --> 00:24:05.910
if you like.

00:24:05.910 --> 00:24:09.450
So collaborating with startups
to help you speed up time

00:24:09.450 --> 00:24:13.830
to market is really a very
powerful, legitimate way

00:24:13.830 --> 00:24:14.850
to act.

00:24:14.850 --> 00:24:19.650
You can buy in and acquire
companies to help you speed up

00:24:19.650 --> 00:24:21.160
your activity.

00:24:21.160 --> 00:24:23.430
And one of the best approaches
that I do a lot of work

00:24:23.430 --> 00:24:26.100
with today is creating
your own external venture

00:24:26.100 --> 00:24:28.650
outside of the
core business where

00:24:28.650 --> 00:24:30.270
you can see a
market opportunity,

00:24:30.270 --> 00:24:33.480
a bit like Arity we
heard from Olly just now.

00:24:33.480 --> 00:24:35.820
But if we try and create that
within the core business,

00:24:35.820 --> 00:24:38.010
we know it's going
to get suffocated.

00:24:38.010 --> 00:24:41.310
Let's create a separate
venture with separate metrics,

00:24:41.310 --> 00:24:45.810
new skills, hire new talent
to drive forward quickly

00:24:45.810 --> 00:24:48.390
and effectively to grab
that market opportunity.

00:24:48.390 --> 00:24:50.880
And that's a
particular capability

00:24:50.880 --> 00:24:54.930
that is really important now
as things are moving so fast.

00:24:54.930 --> 00:24:57.420
And here are a few
examples of companies

00:24:57.420 --> 00:25:01.390
that bring to life some of
those examples I gave you there.

00:25:01.390 --> 00:25:04.350
So I wouldn't go through
it all right now.

00:25:04.350 --> 00:25:06.720
But I'm going to-- let's
just pick on a few here.

00:25:06.720 --> 00:25:09.360
For General Electric,
it decided when

00:25:09.360 --> 00:25:12.120
it was moving to be
a digital company,

00:25:12.120 --> 00:25:13.810
digital industrial
company, it said,

00:25:13.810 --> 00:25:15.630
we just don't have
the skills in-house

00:25:15.630 --> 00:25:17.730
to create the
products and solutions

00:25:17.730 --> 00:25:19.230
that our customers want.

00:25:19.230 --> 00:25:22.950
And they created
a huge marketplace

00:25:22.950 --> 00:25:25.980
to tap into the external
talent outside the walls

00:25:25.980 --> 00:25:27.400
of their company.

00:25:27.400 --> 00:25:29.940
So they could develop
products and services

00:25:29.940 --> 00:25:31.080
for their customers.

00:25:31.080 --> 00:25:33.990
Very powerful, very
practical platform

00:25:33.990 --> 00:25:37.950
strategy, which delivered
about $7 billion of value

00:25:37.950 --> 00:25:40.470
to the organization
over seven years.

00:25:40.470 --> 00:25:43.260
I'm going to pick on a
few examples of companies

00:25:43.260 --> 00:25:46.110
later on as I progress.

00:25:46.110 --> 00:25:47.700
I just want to pick on a few.

00:25:47.700 --> 00:25:50.010
Tinkoff Bank is one of
my favorite examples

00:25:50.010 --> 00:25:52.860
of moments in the
financial services sector.

00:25:52.860 --> 00:25:56.190
And it completely
revolutionized digital banking.

00:25:56.190 --> 00:25:57.870
Started off being
a traditional bank

00:25:57.870 --> 00:26:01.290
and it's created a whole
series of lifestyle ecosystems

00:26:01.290 --> 00:26:03.900
around it and has the highest--

00:26:03.900 --> 00:26:06.510
some of the highest metrics of
the banks in the world today

00:26:06.510 --> 00:26:09.510
in terms of return on equity,
and so on and so forth.

00:26:09.510 --> 00:26:13.740
And in the world of embedding
capabilities into the platform,

00:26:13.740 --> 00:26:16.830
PayPal is obviously a
great example there.

00:26:16.830 --> 00:26:19.275
PayPal enables nearly
every global platform--

00:26:19.275 --> 00:26:22.570
or many global platforms
around the world.

00:26:22.570 --> 00:26:24.810
So let's look at some examples.

00:26:24.810 --> 00:26:27.810
And one of my favorites of
a traditional company that

00:26:27.810 --> 00:26:30.420
has really embraced
platform thinking

00:26:30.420 --> 00:26:33.230
is Ping An, which was--

00:26:33.230 --> 00:26:36.360
and still is-- an
insurance company in China.

00:26:36.360 --> 00:26:38.400
But these pie
charts you see here

00:26:38.400 --> 00:26:42.990
represent how they shifted
or reallocated capital

00:26:42.990 --> 00:26:44.453
to the new type
of business models

00:26:44.453 --> 00:26:45.870
that I've just
been talking about.

00:26:45.870 --> 00:26:49.860
In 2008, they were a
simple insurance company,

00:26:49.860 --> 00:26:51.790
operating with that
type of business model.

00:26:51.790 --> 00:26:55.950
By 2018, they had
reallocated capital,

00:26:55.950 --> 00:26:59.610
management [INAUDIBLE],, and
skill to create a network

00:26:59.610 --> 00:27:03.390
of ecosystem outside of
traditional financial

00:27:03.390 --> 00:27:04.260
services--

00:27:04.260 --> 00:27:07.830
in real estate, in buying and
selling cars, in health care,

00:27:07.830 --> 00:27:09.540
remote health care.

00:27:09.540 --> 00:27:13.470
And those ecosystems
enabled by platforms

00:27:13.470 --> 00:27:17.160
have helped it-- have
helped it to grow way

00:27:17.160 --> 00:27:19.650
beyond their
traditional competitors.

00:27:19.650 --> 00:27:23.100
And I anticipate they'll do
more and more of that over time

00:27:23.100 --> 00:27:28.020
and their business model
portfolio will evolve even more

00:27:28.020 --> 00:27:29.950
to be even more powerful.

00:27:29.950 --> 00:27:33.310
And you can see here, this is
their share price over time

00:27:33.310 --> 00:27:34.340
here.

00:27:34.340 --> 00:27:36.220
They're the blue.

00:27:36.220 --> 00:27:39.940
They're now worth more than
Europe's biggest bank, HSBC.

00:27:39.940 --> 00:27:43.240
HSBC used to own a
large stake in Ping An.

00:27:43.240 --> 00:27:46.675
It sold that stake after
the last financial crisis.

00:27:46.675 --> 00:27:49.720
Ping An has overtaken them,
is much more valuable,

00:27:49.720 --> 00:27:53.080
and now has become the
biggest shareholder,

00:27:53.080 --> 00:27:55.480
independent
shareholder, in HSBC.

00:27:55.480 --> 00:28:00.670
And you can see HSBC's
trajectory compared to theirs.

00:28:00.670 --> 00:28:02.110
And indeed, they
have outperformed

00:28:02.110 --> 00:28:04.510
their local competitors.

00:28:04.510 --> 00:28:08.008
It's not just,
well, that's China.

00:28:08.008 --> 00:28:09.550
The application of
the business model

00:28:09.550 --> 00:28:12.040
has helped them outcompete
Europe's biggest

00:28:12.040 --> 00:28:15.050
bank and the local
competitors, as well.

00:28:15.050 --> 00:28:17.640
And the key thing that has
driven it is leadership.

00:28:17.640 --> 00:28:20.590
The leader said he looked at
how Alibaba and others were

00:28:20.590 --> 00:28:23.290
operating and he said,
we want to emulate that.

00:28:23.290 --> 00:28:25.270
He said, we're no longer
an insurance company.

00:28:25.270 --> 00:28:29.890
We are now going to be a tech
company with financial services

00:28:29.890 --> 00:28:31.250
licenses.

00:28:31.250 --> 00:28:33.370
And they embraced
platform thinking.

00:28:33.370 --> 00:28:36.970
And they've been
incredibly successful.

00:28:36.970 --> 00:28:41.440
We'll come back and talk about
them maybe later on today.

00:28:41.440 --> 00:28:43.417
So what does this
mean for leaders

00:28:43.417 --> 00:28:44.500
and traditional companies?

00:28:44.500 --> 00:28:47.140
Well, I wrote a book on
this called "FightBack."

00:28:47.140 --> 00:28:49.900
And it's as much
about fighting back

00:28:49.900 --> 00:28:52.210
against old ways of
thinking and acting

00:28:52.210 --> 00:28:55.720
as it is against fighting back
against digital disruption.

00:28:55.720 --> 00:28:59.800
And there are five key steps
to renew an incumbent business

00:28:59.800 --> 00:29:02.860
model, to make it
fit for the future.

00:29:02.860 --> 00:29:05.530
And particularly in
these times of crisis,

00:29:05.530 --> 00:29:09.520
the imperative to apply these
principles is really strong.

00:29:09.520 --> 00:29:12.038
And the first thing
is, we need to reset

00:29:12.038 --> 00:29:14.080
the way our leaders think
about growth and value.

00:29:14.080 --> 00:29:15.630
Most leaders of
incumbent businesses

00:29:15.630 --> 00:29:18.580
have become successful
operating a certain type

00:29:18.580 --> 00:29:19.720
of business model.

00:29:19.720 --> 00:29:21.760
And the digital business
models that we've

00:29:21.760 --> 00:29:26.060
been talking about just now
are often alien and different.

00:29:26.060 --> 00:29:29.410
We need to rethink about the
ecosystems that are emerging

00:29:29.410 --> 00:29:32.912
and where we could play
and our roles within that.

00:29:32.912 --> 00:29:35.120
And this comes back to the
way of executing, as well.

00:29:35.120 --> 00:29:37.030
We can't just try and
execute everything

00:29:37.030 --> 00:29:38.510
from within the core business.

00:29:38.510 --> 00:29:42.310
We need to reallocate capital,
as we heard from Ping An,

00:29:42.310 --> 00:29:44.500
in a different way,
and put our money

00:29:44.500 --> 00:29:48.010
where our mouth is if we really
want to be a digital player.

00:29:48.010 --> 00:29:50.380
And probably the
most crucial thing--

00:29:50.380 --> 00:29:53.560
we cannot do disruptive
innovation from within the core

00:29:53.560 --> 00:29:54.500
business.

00:29:54.500 --> 00:29:56.140
It rarely works.

00:29:56.140 --> 00:29:59.050
We need to create an
ambidextrous, two-handed

00:29:59.050 --> 00:30:01.960
organization and
provide the space

00:30:01.960 --> 00:30:04.450
for new digital businesses
to take off, like we heard

00:30:04.450 --> 00:30:07.120
from Arity from earlier on.

00:30:07.120 --> 00:30:10.390
They can access the assets
of the core business,

00:30:10.390 --> 00:30:13.910
but they have different
[INAUDIBLE],, different metrics,

00:30:13.910 --> 00:30:16.310
different space in
which they can operate,

00:30:16.310 --> 00:30:18.310
often equity-based ventures.

00:30:18.310 --> 00:30:19.690
This is how Ping An did it.

00:30:19.690 --> 00:30:22.900
It created separate
equity based ventures,

00:30:22.900 --> 00:30:25.810
which attracted A-grade
entrepreneurs to drive them

00:30:25.810 --> 00:30:29.860
forward but connected them
back to the core business

00:30:29.860 --> 00:30:31.880
and allowed the core
business to control them.

00:30:31.880 --> 00:30:34.750
They weren't third
party investments.

00:30:34.750 --> 00:30:36.610
And then finally, if
we do this, we can,

00:30:36.610 --> 00:30:38.920
like [INAUDIBLE] have
done, is widen our scope,

00:30:38.920 --> 00:30:42.160
be more involved in more
parts that our customers like,

00:30:42.160 --> 00:30:46.420
and deliver much
more value to them.

00:30:46.420 --> 00:30:51.040
Now, we'll end with one tip
and a couple of case studies.

00:30:51.040 --> 00:30:53.020
And then we'll open
up for a discussion.

00:30:53.020 --> 00:30:54.910
One of the most
challenging things

00:30:54.910 --> 00:30:57.850
when you are trying to describe
these types of new business

00:30:57.850 --> 00:31:02.170
models is making it tangible
for the leaders of the business

00:31:02.170 --> 00:31:03.950
units in the core business.

00:31:03.950 --> 00:31:05.750
They have all the power.

00:31:05.750 --> 00:31:07.130
They run the business.

00:31:07.130 --> 00:31:08.410
They have the budgets.

00:31:08.410 --> 00:31:10.330
And they have all
the assets that you

00:31:10.330 --> 00:31:12.420
would love to leverage.

00:31:12.420 --> 00:31:14.550
And one thing I
find very effective

00:31:14.550 --> 00:31:19.140
is to sit down with the leaders
of the existing business units

00:31:19.140 --> 00:31:23.650
and show how, by creating
digital platform businesses,

00:31:23.650 --> 00:31:26.520
whether they're internal or
they're separate ventures,

00:31:26.520 --> 00:31:30.930
we can increase the
revenue or the core metrics

00:31:30.930 --> 00:31:33.360
that are important
for the core business

00:31:33.360 --> 00:31:34.860
by significant amounts.

00:31:34.860 --> 00:31:38.100
And we can show how we can do
that to defend their business,

00:31:38.100 --> 00:31:39.950
create new revenue.

00:31:39.950 --> 00:31:42.660
What are the metrics that
are important to them?

00:31:42.660 --> 00:31:45.750
And here's an example I worked
on with an insurance company

00:31:45.750 --> 00:31:50.340
to show over five years how
we could create a material

00:31:50.340 --> 00:31:54.720
difference to their business by
applying the principles I just

00:31:54.720 --> 00:31:56.680
described today.

00:31:56.680 --> 00:31:58.960
And I'll give you a
couple of examples here,

00:31:58.960 --> 00:32:01.365
how it is possible for any--

00:32:01.365 --> 00:32:04.300
my belief-- any
business in any sector

00:32:04.300 --> 00:32:06.460
to create a
significant new growth

00:32:06.460 --> 00:32:09.700
path if they can
remove the blinkers

00:32:09.700 --> 00:32:13.110
and apply some of the principles
and methods I talked about.

00:32:13.110 --> 00:32:17.140
And FLSmidth is a very
large mining company

00:32:17.140 --> 00:32:18.478
that wanted to say--

00:32:18.478 --> 00:32:20.020
wanted you to ask
yourself, how do we

00:32:20.020 --> 00:32:24.620
create 20% net new growth
over the next five years?

00:32:24.620 --> 00:32:27.760
And by applying the
principles I talked about,

00:32:27.760 --> 00:32:32.230
envisaging new ecosystems
and spotting opportunities

00:32:32.230 --> 00:32:34.900
to apply those
different platform

00:32:34.900 --> 00:32:38.110
plays and the different
ways of executing,

00:32:38.110 --> 00:32:41.080
they identified $3 billion
of new growth opportunity

00:32:41.080 --> 00:32:43.050
over the next five years.

00:32:43.050 --> 00:32:45.060
Now, the other example
I want to share with you

00:32:45.060 --> 00:32:48.540
is a small insurance
company that said,

00:32:48.540 --> 00:32:51.180
we need to be engaging
with millennials.

00:32:51.180 --> 00:32:52.260
They don't buy insurance.

00:32:52.260 --> 00:32:53.650
They don't look to the future.

00:32:53.650 --> 00:32:56.505
We know that they
will be most of-- they

00:32:56.505 --> 00:32:59.790
will make up the majority of the
insurance market in a few years

00:32:59.790 --> 00:33:00.545
time.

00:33:00.545 --> 00:33:01.670
How do we engage with them?

00:33:01.670 --> 00:33:03.170
We don't have the
skills to do that.

00:33:03.170 --> 00:33:06.030
And they created a
separate joint venture

00:33:06.030 --> 00:33:09.200
with entrepreneurs with
separate equity structure--

00:33:09.200 --> 00:33:13.000
separate equity structure
to tackle that market.

00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:15.150
And that's been
incredibly successful.

00:33:15.150 --> 00:33:18.090
And they're on track
for a significant impact

00:33:18.090 --> 00:33:19.410
to their core business.

00:33:19.410 --> 00:33:21.120
And they've done
it at record speed,

00:33:21.120 --> 00:33:22.860
something that
would be impossible

00:33:22.860 --> 00:33:24.210
within the core business.

00:33:24.210 --> 00:33:27.720
And then finally,
probably the best example

00:33:27.720 --> 00:33:30.870
of the ambidextrous organization
being put in place--

00:33:30.870 --> 00:33:34.770
BP, knowing that
fundamentally fossil fuels

00:33:34.770 --> 00:33:37.270
are under significant threat
over the next-- no one

00:33:37.270 --> 00:33:42.150
wants fossil fuels over
the next 10, 20 years.

00:33:42.150 --> 00:33:44.170
They said, we cannot innovate.

00:33:44.170 --> 00:33:46.050
We need to change
the way we innovate.

00:33:46.050 --> 00:33:47.700
It's not making an impact.

00:33:47.700 --> 00:33:52.290
They created a separate unit
called Launchpad in which they

00:33:52.290 --> 00:33:54.960
said, we're going to create
five unicorn businesses

00:33:54.960 --> 00:33:57.950
over the next five years,
five unicorn businesses

00:33:57.950 --> 00:34:00.690
so that [INAUDIBLE]
those businesses need

00:34:00.690 --> 00:34:06.070
to add 5% to the EBITDA of
our core business, as well.

00:34:06.070 --> 00:34:08.340
So they created a
future business metric

00:34:08.340 --> 00:34:12.030
and they said, to engage the
leaders of the core business,

00:34:12.030 --> 00:34:15.860
we are going to add value to
you over this period, as well.

00:34:15.860 --> 00:34:18.900
This is not innovation theater
something a long way away

00:34:18.900 --> 00:34:19.500
from the core.

00:34:19.500 --> 00:34:22.860
This is about-- we're
changing the whole business

00:34:22.860 --> 00:34:26.370
so we can be [INAUDIBLE] for
a different sort of future.

00:34:26.370 --> 00:34:29.219
They put their
deputy CEO in charge

00:34:29.219 --> 00:34:31.860
and it's one of
the best examples

00:34:31.860 --> 00:34:34.889
I can see today
of really applying

00:34:34.889 --> 00:34:39.380
the ambidextrous approach
and being committed to it.

00:34:39.380 --> 00:34:43.449
So thank you very much for
your attention this morning

00:34:43.449 --> 00:34:47.050
and looking forward to
discussing this with you.

00:34:47.050 --> 00:34:49.170
It's all about
how we can release

00:34:49.170 --> 00:34:53.530
the inner digital Superman
from the wonderful assets

00:34:53.530 --> 00:34:55.690
and capabilities
we have, which tend

00:34:55.690 --> 00:35:01.780
to be locked inside our Clark
Kent, which exemplifies how

00:35:01.780 --> 00:35:05.710
most businesses, incumbent
businesses, operate and think

00:35:05.710 --> 00:35:06.770
today.

00:35:06.770 --> 00:35:09.110
Thank you.

00:35:09.110 --> 00:35:10.480
OLIVER OGG: Thank you, Simon.

00:35:10.480 --> 00:35:16.250
And that's many fabulous
insights shared there.

00:35:16.250 --> 00:35:19.420
So this is our opportunity
now to take some questions

00:35:19.420 --> 00:35:20.630
from the audience.

00:35:20.630 --> 00:35:22.900
First of all, from Roland--

00:35:22.900 --> 00:35:26.140
there's a perspective--
sorry, from your perspective

00:35:26.140 --> 00:35:29.080
of working amongst
European corporates,

00:35:29.080 --> 00:35:32.080
do you think too many are
waiting for or expecting

00:35:32.080 --> 00:35:36.400
the EU to assist either
financially with innovation

00:35:36.400 --> 00:35:38.770
or controlling through
regulation, as opposed

00:35:38.770 --> 00:35:41.140
to corporates
actually being braver

00:35:41.140 --> 00:35:43.720
and starting out and perhaps
leveraging a first mover

00:35:43.720 --> 00:35:44.740
advantage?

00:35:44.740 --> 00:35:47.300
I think Simon would be
best to answer that.

00:35:47.300 --> 00:35:48.390
SIMON TORRANCE: Yeah, it's
a really good question.

00:35:48.390 --> 00:35:50.432
So obviously we don't want
to wait for regulators

00:35:50.432 --> 00:35:53.370
to do anything, because that
would be a recipe for disaster.

00:35:53.370 --> 00:35:58.550
However-- however-- we do
need to help regulators create

00:35:58.550 --> 00:36:00.350
the right regulatory
environments, which

00:36:00.350 --> 00:36:02.030
are fair and reasonable.

00:36:02.030 --> 00:36:03.920
And we need to help
governments, as well,

00:36:03.920 --> 00:36:06.680
create the right
industrial structures,

00:36:06.680 --> 00:36:11.138
for particularly European
economy to thrive.

00:36:11.138 --> 00:36:12.680
And the problem we
have at the moment

00:36:12.680 --> 00:36:17.390
is that because traditional
European corporates are not

00:36:17.390 --> 00:36:19.670
so sophisticated about
the digital world

00:36:19.670 --> 00:36:22.550
as some of the big
digital giants,

00:36:22.550 --> 00:36:27.140
their lobbying skills
are less powerful.

00:36:27.140 --> 00:36:32.990
And so we need to upgrade
our knowledge of how

00:36:32.990 --> 00:36:35.360
digital business models,
like the ones I've mentioned,

00:36:35.360 --> 00:36:39.410
operate, so that we as
industrial European companies

00:36:39.410 --> 00:36:44.420
can lobby more effectively and
help governments and regulators

00:36:44.420 --> 00:36:49.610
create more effective structures
for innovation to happen.

00:36:49.610 --> 00:36:52.320
And it's quite interesting.

00:36:52.320 --> 00:36:57.020
In the financial sector,
PSD2 has been a regulation

00:36:57.020 --> 00:37:00.440
to open up our data,
customer data, from banks

00:37:00.440 --> 00:37:02.540
to allow people to
innovate on top of it.

00:37:02.540 --> 00:37:06.470
And that's created a whole
flurry of wonderful innovation

00:37:06.470 --> 00:37:08.340
in the fintech world.

00:37:08.340 --> 00:37:11.240
And we may well see
that type of regulation

00:37:11.240 --> 00:37:17.990
applied to the big tech giants
to force them to open up access

00:37:17.990 --> 00:37:18.600
to data.

00:37:18.600 --> 00:37:20.600
That's coming through at
the moment through data

00:37:20.600 --> 00:37:21.650
portability.

00:37:21.650 --> 00:37:24.800
So we need to--
absolutely, we need

00:37:24.800 --> 00:37:27.540
to take the bull by the horns
and innovate for ourselves.

00:37:27.540 --> 00:37:30.170
And I think we need a more
sophisticated discussion

00:37:30.170 --> 00:37:33.763
with government
regulators, as well.

00:37:33.763 --> 00:37:34.680
OLIVER OGG: Fantastic.

00:37:34.680 --> 00:37:37.880
And a great question
here from Dorita--

00:37:37.880 --> 00:37:41.070
apologies if I've not
said that correctly.

00:37:41.070 --> 00:37:43.920
Do you always need to have a
visionary leader at the top?

00:37:43.920 --> 00:37:45.620
Otherwise, how do
you approach making

00:37:45.620 --> 00:37:48.050
a case for the investment
into a transformation

00:37:48.050 --> 00:37:51.020
into a platform
business where the ROI

00:37:51.020 --> 00:37:52.880
is difficult to estimate?

00:37:52.880 --> 00:37:55.580
And in some business
cultures, financial numbers

00:37:55.580 --> 00:37:57.620
have to stack up front.

00:37:57.620 --> 00:37:58.550
Very interesting.

00:37:58.550 --> 00:38:02.870
And by the way, I was on a
conversation with a cohort

00:38:02.870 --> 00:38:05.490
of banking leaders yesterday.

00:38:05.490 --> 00:38:07.640
And we were talking
about DBS in Singapore,

00:38:07.640 --> 00:38:13.460
who launched 150 APIs as
part of their go to market.

00:38:13.460 --> 00:38:15.863
And the position there
is that they don't

00:38:15.863 --> 00:38:17.030
want to be front and center.

00:38:17.030 --> 00:38:18.410
They don't want to be
owning all these channels.

00:38:18.410 --> 00:38:19.950
They want to be invisible.

00:38:19.950 --> 00:38:22.520
And that's their strategy.

00:38:22.520 --> 00:38:25.040
And I can't imagine there
was ever a proposed business

00:38:25.040 --> 00:38:27.580
case with hard numbers
behind something like that.

00:38:27.580 --> 00:38:30.322
But Simon, any thoughts?

00:38:30.322 --> 00:38:32.030
SIMON TORRANCE: Well,
you're quite right.

00:38:32.030 --> 00:38:33.150
The question is a very good one.

00:38:33.150 --> 00:38:35.570
So sometimes the business
does have a visionary leader.

00:38:35.570 --> 00:38:37.500
Now, the danger
sometimes is that leader

00:38:37.500 --> 00:38:38.870
can be too visionary.

00:38:38.870 --> 00:38:42.410
And if they don't pull along
with them, the shareholders

00:38:42.410 --> 00:38:44.300
and the rest of
the organization,

00:38:44.300 --> 00:38:46.550
that can cause a lot of trouble.

00:38:46.550 --> 00:38:49.370
In the case of Ping An,
the leader is a visionary.

00:38:49.370 --> 00:38:51.470
And he's the founder
of the company.

00:38:51.470 --> 00:38:52.830
He's an entrepreneur.

00:38:52.830 --> 00:38:54.710
So for him, he's
got that power--

00:38:54.710 --> 00:38:56.900
like Jeff Bezos-- to
make things happen.

00:38:56.900 --> 00:39:01.310
But most traditional, industrial
companies don't necessarily

00:39:01.310 --> 00:39:02.875
have that in place.

00:39:02.875 --> 00:39:04.250
And we tend to
have what we might

00:39:04.250 --> 00:39:07.520
call caretaker managers, who
are very successful operators

00:39:07.520 --> 00:39:09.290
of existing business models.

00:39:09.290 --> 00:39:13.040
And their job tends to
be very focused on what

00:39:13.040 --> 00:39:16.980
the shareholders or the pension
funds are acquiring from them.

00:39:16.980 --> 00:39:20.510
So we need some
parallel activity.

00:39:20.510 --> 00:39:22.610
We do need a new discussion
with the shareholders

00:39:22.610 --> 00:39:25.130
to allow us to do new things.

00:39:25.130 --> 00:39:29.450
But probably first we need
to prove that new business

00:39:29.450 --> 00:39:31.340
models can deliver value.

00:39:31.340 --> 00:39:34.280
And that's why I was
saying before one

00:39:34.280 --> 00:39:37.670
of the good techniques is to
work with the existing business

00:39:37.670 --> 00:39:42.680
leaders to show how the
platform activity can

00:39:42.680 --> 00:39:45.110
deliver a meaningful impact.

00:39:45.110 --> 00:39:49.430
And that's a difficult thing
to do, but it can be done.

00:39:49.430 --> 00:39:52.620
And it's an important
part of the process.

00:39:52.620 --> 00:39:54.590
Now, occasionally you
might say, well, it's

00:39:54.590 --> 00:39:56.130
a no brainer to do APIs.

00:39:56.130 --> 00:39:58.520
And I think that is
correct, because it

00:39:58.520 --> 00:40:00.080
can facilitate so many things.

00:40:00.080 --> 00:40:02.210
And it's not a huge cost.

00:40:02.210 --> 00:40:04.580
But to really get the
organization to move forward,

00:40:04.580 --> 00:40:09.170
you need to have a grown up
discussion showing practically

00:40:09.170 --> 00:40:12.950
how platform business models
can affect the core metrics.

00:40:12.950 --> 00:40:15.470
And indeed, one of the
best ways of doing that

00:40:15.470 --> 00:40:17.600
is to create external
ventures where

00:40:17.600 --> 00:40:20.870
you can fast track something
quickly into the market.

00:40:20.870 --> 00:40:23.030
And that demonstrates
the art of the possible.

00:40:23.030 --> 00:40:24.670
We've now done it three times--

00:40:24.670 --> 00:40:27.260
like the example from
the Danish insurer there.

00:40:27.260 --> 00:40:28.550
We've created this venture.

00:40:28.550 --> 00:40:30.810
And already it's
worth a lot of money.

00:40:30.810 --> 00:40:33.110
It's delivering a lot of value.

00:40:33.110 --> 00:40:35.870
Wouldn't it be great if we
could do 10 more of these?

00:40:35.870 --> 00:40:39.920
And that demonstrates the
art of the possible-- help

00:40:39.920 --> 00:40:42.770
the leaders of the core
business become more confident.

00:40:42.770 --> 00:40:46.050
And it's something they can
show to the shareholders

00:40:46.050 --> 00:40:47.690
to illustrate what can be done.

00:40:50.300 --> 00:40:53.004
OLIVER OGG: Fantastic.

00:40:53.004 --> 00:40:55.513
So let's [INAUDIBLE]
chat between ourselves.

00:40:55.513 --> 00:40:58.180
But I am keeping an eye on these
questions as they flow through.

00:40:58.180 --> 00:41:02.240
And I'm going to lean on some of
these, because they're spot on.

00:41:02.240 --> 00:41:05.380
And I think that point
about executive leadership,

00:41:05.380 --> 00:41:10.220
but also proving [INAUDIBLE]
is always hot topics.

00:41:10.220 --> 00:41:15.250
I mean, I think even pre-COVID.

00:41:15.250 --> 00:41:17.658
There's another question
actually about quick wins

00:41:17.658 --> 00:41:18.700
from a platform strategy.

00:41:18.700 --> 00:41:20.325
I think you've kind
of touched on that,

00:41:20.325 --> 00:41:22.960
how to demonstrate something
relatively quickly within.

00:41:22.960 --> 00:41:28.440
And this individual's asking
between [INAUDIBLE] pretty

00:41:28.440 --> 00:41:29.037
tight.

00:41:29.037 --> 00:41:30.870
And one of the things
I've certainly learned

00:41:30.870 --> 00:41:33.330
is a lot of
businesses are already

00:41:33.330 --> 00:41:35.730
trying to work efficiently,
or more efficiently,

00:41:35.730 --> 00:41:37.420
with partners.

00:41:37.420 --> 00:41:42.600
So perhaps let's not focus
on unknown innovation first.

00:41:42.600 --> 00:41:46.800
Let's perhaps think about, who
are the partners that you're

00:41:46.800 --> 00:41:48.590
working with or work
with imminently,

00:41:48.590 --> 00:41:50.810
and let's do this in
and interesting ways.

00:41:50.810 --> 00:41:55.820
And there I think more immediate
things can be shown at pace.

00:41:55.820 --> 00:41:58.610
I mean, Simon, any
reflections on that comment?

00:41:58.610 --> 00:42:01.193
SIMON TORRANCE: Well, you can,
but that's sort of incremental.

00:42:01.193 --> 00:42:03.470
I mean, it's necessary
but insufficient.

00:42:03.470 --> 00:42:04.992
It's not going to
move the needle.

00:42:04.992 --> 00:42:06.450
And you've seen--
I just showed you

00:42:06.450 --> 00:42:08.333
the share prices of the DAX.

00:42:08.333 --> 00:42:09.500
It's the same with the FTSE.

00:42:09.500 --> 00:42:11.842
All incumbent businesses
are not doing well.

00:42:11.842 --> 00:42:13.550
The ones that are
doing well are the ones

00:42:13.550 --> 00:42:17.630
that have invested in
platform business models.

00:42:17.630 --> 00:42:20.870
And Apple and [INAUDIBLE]
didn't start out with that.

00:42:20.870 --> 00:42:25.260
They added it to their business
model 10 or 20 years ago.

00:42:25.260 --> 00:42:28.250
So absolutely you need
to be more efficient.

00:42:28.250 --> 00:42:31.280
You need to improve the way you
serve your existing companies

00:42:31.280 --> 00:42:33.430
and optimize your
existing business model.

00:42:33.430 --> 00:42:34.020
Absolutely.

00:42:34.020 --> 00:42:35.335
That's critical.

00:42:35.335 --> 00:42:37.460
But if you don't do the
other thing, the other part

00:42:37.460 --> 00:42:39.590
of the ambidextrous
activity, you're

00:42:39.590 --> 00:42:44.840
just in a race with your
competitors getting nowhere.

00:42:44.840 --> 00:42:47.900
So you do need to do
both of these things.

00:42:47.900 --> 00:42:52.010
And rather than put virtually
no investments on this side,

00:42:52.010 --> 00:42:53.270
or a tiny bit of--

00:42:53.270 --> 00:42:56.120
let's throw a bit of money
to the innovation team.

00:42:56.120 --> 00:42:59.270
Like BP has done, you
need to say, right,

00:42:59.270 --> 00:43:01.340
we're going to put
$500 million here

00:43:01.340 --> 00:43:03.770
to create five
unicorns, while also

00:43:03.770 --> 00:43:07.190
driving value for the key
metric, which is the EBITDA

00:43:07.190 --> 00:43:08.210
margin.

00:43:08.210 --> 00:43:11.520
And that is a very bold and
the most appropriate approach,

00:43:11.520 --> 00:43:14.870
doing both activities
and committing

00:43:14.870 --> 00:43:17.710
to both at the same time.

00:43:17.710 --> 00:43:21.030
OLIVER OGG: And
with Launch Pad--

00:43:21.030 --> 00:43:22.580
so significant ambition there.

00:43:22.580 --> 00:43:29.750
But are any of those
ventures likely to erode any

00:43:29.750 --> 00:43:30.917
of the existing [INAUDIBLE]?

00:43:30.917 --> 00:43:33.125
Because I think one of the
challenges I've often seen

00:43:33.125 --> 00:43:34.850
is that classic
innovator's dilemma

00:43:34.850 --> 00:43:38.610
where there's a new
venture being proposed.

00:43:38.610 --> 00:43:42.230
There's certainly interest
and excitement around it.

00:43:42.230 --> 00:43:44.960
But there's concern
that ultimately it's

00:43:44.960 --> 00:43:48.890
going to disrupt a revenue line
that actually might be quite

00:43:48.890 --> 00:43:51.710
profitable, even when they
know that that revenue is

00:43:51.710 --> 00:43:54.390
potentially at risk,
if not at risk.

00:43:54.390 --> 00:43:55.793
SIMON TORRANCE: Yeah.

00:43:55.793 --> 00:43:57.710
So it's a very good
question, and particularly

00:43:57.710 --> 00:43:59.780
in the retail sector,
when retailers

00:43:59.780 --> 00:44:02.690
think about creating
online marketplaces, which

00:44:02.690 --> 00:44:05.570
is essentially allowing
third parties--

00:44:05.570 --> 00:44:08.000
maybe competitors, as
well-- to sell through you

00:44:08.000 --> 00:44:09.200
to your customers.

00:44:09.200 --> 00:44:11.160
And even Amazon have
this debate internally.

00:44:11.160 --> 00:44:12.920
Should we open up
to third parties

00:44:12.920 --> 00:44:17.330
rather than buying the inventory
ourselves and sell it all?

00:44:17.330 --> 00:44:20.840
And they had the foresight
to look ahead and said, well,

00:44:20.840 --> 00:44:22.280
what do our customers
really want?

00:44:22.280 --> 00:44:25.520
Let's think about our
customer, not about us.

00:44:25.520 --> 00:44:27.710
And our customers want
choice and a selection.

00:44:27.710 --> 00:44:28.650
And we can't.

00:44:28.650 --> 00:44:32.660
We don't have the
money or the capability

00:44:32.660 --> 00:44:35.960
to provide everything for
our customers ourselves.

00:44:35.960 --> 00:44:39.770
So by looking ahead, focusing
on what customers needed,

00:44:39.770 --> 00:44:42.650
and understanding how the
internet and digital technology

00:44:42.650 --> 00:44:47.440
could enable that through APIs
20 years ago, as you said,

00:44:47.440 --> 00:44:50.450
they said, well, let's
believe in this vision

00:44:50.450 --> 00:44:51.680
and make it happen.

00:44:51.680 --> 00:44:53.840
Now, in practice,
how you execute

00:44:53.840 --> 00:44:55.600
that needs to be
done very carefully

00:44:55.600 --> 00:45:00.140
and the third party partners
or complementary service

00:45:00.140 --> 00:45:02.150
providers you choose
to start with,

00:45:02.150 --> 00:45:05.210
you might choose those that
are not directly competitive.

00:45:05.210 --> 00:45:08.330
And over time, as you're
growing the knowledge and skills

00:45:08.330 --> 00:45:12.150
of how to curate a marketplace,
then you might open up further.

00:45:12.150 --> 00:45:14.720
And that's what a lot
of the second wave

00:45:14.720 --> 00:45:18.710
of traditional retailers
have learned how to do.

00:45:18.710 --> 00:45:23.390
So yes, there is a potential
threat of cannibalization.

00:45:23.390 --> 00:45:26.930
But it can easily be managed if
you think it through properly.

00:45:26.930 --> 00:45:31.250
And indeed, in the case of
BP, none of those businesses

00:45:31.250 --> 00:45:33.780
are threatening
the core business.

00:45:33.780 --> 00:45:37.250
In fact, as I said, they
have been explicitly set up

00:45:37.250 --> 00:45:42.270
to drive 5% EBITDA improvement
to the core business.

00:45:42.270 --> 00:45:44.090
So it's actually the opposite.

00:45:44.090 --> 00:45:48.665
They're designed specifically
to deliver that value there as

00:45:48.665 --> 00:45:50.557
well as separate
value for themselves.

00:45:52.560 --> 00:45:53.310
OLIVER OGG: Great.

00:45:53.310 --> 00:45:54.470
Thank you.

00:45:54.470 --> 00:46:01.180
You talked about the value that
incumbents might be sitting on.

00:46:01.180 --> 00:46:03.070
And the danger, I
guess, is that there

00:46:03.070 --> 00:46:06.910
can be an ego around the brand.

00:46:06.910 --> 00:46:08.980
And actually, I
just talked about--

00:46:08.980 --> 00:46:11.697
I just mentioned
that example of DHS,

00:46:11.697 --> 00:46:13.030
where they want to be invisible.

00:46:13.030 --> 00:46:15.070
I mean, that's quite an
extraordinary statement

00:46:15.070 --> 00:46:18.370
for a bank to make,
where often they

00:46:18.370 --> 00:46:19.580
want to be front and center.

00:46:19.580 --> 00:46:22.360
You know, the brand is a
compelling gravitational pull.

00:46:24.880 --> 00:46:26.880
Everybody wants to kind
of own that relationship

00:46:26.880 --> 00:46:28.680
with a customer.

00:46:28.680 --> 00:46:33.030
What do you think are the
assets for an incumbent

00:46:33.030 --> 00:46:36.030
and what are the
liabilities for an incumbent

00:46:36.030 --> 00:46:38.305
when thinking of a
platform strategy?

00:46:38.305 --> 00:46:39.180
SIMON TORRANCE: Yeah.

00:46:39.180 --> 00:46:40.750
It's a very good question.

00:46:40.750 --> 00:46:45.400
So again, what you need to do
is have a vision of the future.

00:46:45.400 --> 00:46:48.030
So if you don't have
that and you're always

00:46:48.030 --> 00:46:50.070
looking at the
present or the past

00:46:50.070 --> 00:46:52.180
and trying to extrapolate
from the past,

00:46:52.180 --> 00:46:54.930
then you tend to be trapped
in the mindsets which

00:46:54.930 --> 00:46:57.010
are not going to be helpful.

00:46:57.010 --> 00:46:59.010
So as you say,
banks often say, we

00:46:59.010 --> 00:47:01.170
want to own the
customer relationship.

00:47:01.170 --> 00:47:03.010
We want-- it's our ego.

00:47:03.010 --> 00:47:07.860
We want everybody to come
through us for the services.

00:47:07.860 --> 00:47:10.590
And that's pretty dangerous.

00:47:10.590 --> 00:47:15.570
If, as opposed to that,
you look into the future

00:47:15.570 --> 00:47:21.780
and, for example, you see
that in five or 10 years time,

00:47:21.780 --> 00:47:24.900
platforms are going
to be mediating

00:47:24.900 --> 00:47:29.310
30% of all economic
activity-- about $60 trillion.

00:47:29.310 --> 00:47:31.590
So rather than you trying
to compete with people

00:47:31.590 --> 00:47:33.540
who are much better
at managing customer

00:47:33.540 --> 00:47:36.570
relationships, like
DBS has done, you say,

00:47:36.570 --> 00:47:38.550
why don't we enable it?

00:47:38.550 --> 00:47:41.580
Why don't we embed
our capabilities

00:47:41.580 --> 00:47:44.650
into other people's businesses?

00:47:44.650 --> 00:47:48.040
And in fact, just
this is one example.

00:47:48.040 --> 00:47:51.210
The market for what's
called embedded finance--

00:47:51.210 --> 00:47:53.340
so that's financial
services embedded

00:47:53.340 --> 00:47:55.320
at the point of purchase.

00:47:55.320 --> 00:47:59.130
If you want to go
get an online MBA,

00:47:59.130 --> 00:48:01.575
you want to get a loan
right there and then

00:48:01.575 --> 00:48:03.200
that can be given to
you there and then

00:48:03.200 --> 00:48:06.360
without having to call up a
bank and try and sort it out,

00:48:06.360 --> 00:48:08.160
or you're seeing more
of this in terms of,

00:48:08.160 --> 00:48:10.470
you want to buy
a product but you

00:48:10.470 --> 00:48:14.500
want to pay for it in
installments over a year.

00:48:14.500 --> 00:48:17.550
And this market of
embedded finance--

00:48:17.550 --> 00:48:20.080
I did some analysis of
this the other day--

00:48:20.080 --> 00:48:23.100
it is potentially more
valuable than the whole

00:48:23.100 --> 00:48:25.800
of the global software
market in five or 10 years

00:48:25.800 --> 00:48:28.823
time, because
everything is being--

00:48:28.823 --> 00:48:32.460
there's an explosion
of digital services.

00:48:32.460 --> 00:48:35.940
And we all need to pay
for them or exchange value

00:48:35.940 --> 00:48:37.120
related to them.

00:48:37.120 --> 00:48:39.690
And so it's a clever
move by DBS to say,

00:48:39.690 --> 00:48:42.620
actually, we can see the future.

00:48:42.620 --> 00:48:45.690
And we want to enable it
being in the background

00:48:45.690 --> 00:48:47.850
rather than trying to
compete with companies

00:48:47.850 --> 00:48:50.730
who are much better at
controlling the customer

00:48:50.730 --> 00:48:51.940
interface.

00:48:51.940 --> 00:48:55.260
Now, that's one strategy.

00:48:55.260 --> 00:48:57.420
Ping An has taken a
slightly different strategy.

00:48:57.420 --> 00:49:01.350
And both of these
can be complementary.

00:49:01.350 --> 00:49:04.200
If I was talking to a
bank, I'd say do both.

00:49:04.200 --> 00:49:08.318
Ping An said, we want to
create deeper relationships

00:49:08.318 --> 00:49:09.110
with our customers.

00:49:09.110 --> 00:49:12.690
So let's create some
platforms and ecosystems

00:49:12.690 --> 00:49:15.000
in areas of their
lifestyle that we normally

00:49:15.000 --> 00:49:19.080
don't participate in terms
of buying and selling cars.

00:49:19.080 --> 00:49:21.360
Let's create a
marketplace for that.

00:49:21.360 --> 00:49:25.830
Or a remote health care
telemedicine platform,

00:49:25.830 --> 00:49:28.910
which has obviously taken
off like crazy with COVID.

00:49:28.910 --> 00:49:31.830
And they created these
separate ventures just

00:49:31.830 --> 00:49:34.230
for the opposite
reason of DBS, so they

00:49:34.230 --> 00:49:37.320
could be at the center
of those experiences

00:49:37.320 --> 00:49:41.330
and those could act as a channel
for their financial services.

00:49:41.330 --> 00:49:43.950
But interestingly, they created
separate brands for those,

00:49:43.950 --> 00:49:46.680
because they knew that
those brands needed to be--

00:49:46.680 --> 00:49:49.380
people didn't affiliate Ping
An with buying and selling cars

00:49:49.380 --> 00:49:50.170
and so on.

00:49:50.170 --> 00:49:52.560
So they created separate
entities, separate brands.

00:49:52.560 --> 00:49:54.030
They control them.

00:49:54.030 --> 00:49:56.490
Some of those they've IPOed.

00:49:56.490 --> 00:49:59.445
But they've created, as a
result, this incredible network

00:49:59.445 --> 00:50:01.740
or ecosystem of
platforms that are all

00:50:01.740 --> 00:50:05.580
connected by one single
sign on mechanism

00:50:05.580 --> 00:50:08.640
and an API structure
behind it that allows them

00:50:08.640 --> 00:50:11.090
to intermesh across all of it.

00:50:11.090 --> 00:50:13.200
That's probably one
of the best examples

00:50:13.200 --> 00:50:18.770
of tackling the question
you just asked there.

00:50:18.770 --> 00:50:21.750
OLIVER OGG: I often like
that example from Arity

00:50:21.750 --> 00:50:24.360
where they are now
signing up competitors

00:50:24.360 --> 00:50:29.220
to their parent because they
realized-- they've recognized

00:50:29.220 --> 00:50:31.740
this for years,
but more data means

00:50:31.740 --> 00:50:34.410
they can provide more insights,
which means more value, which

00:50:34.410 --> 00:50:36.160
makes them just more
compelling, this kind

00:50:36.160 --> 00:50:39.300
of gravitational pull,
flywheel effect around data.

00:50:39.300 --> 00:50:40.890
And I guess we often
talk about data

00:50:40.890 --> 00:50:42.270
being the new world as a cliche.

00:50:42.270 --> 00:50:47.900
But it's that combination
of data and models, so i.e.

00:50:47.900 --> 00:50:50.225
the automatic insight
being generated out

00:50:50.225 --> 00:50:53.100
of the vast amounts
of data is going

00:50:53.100 --> 00:50:57.540
to be their defensive
wall as they open up

00:50:57.540 --> 00:51:00.360
and emerge into all these
other different touch

00:51:00.360 --> 00:51:03.240
points and industries.

00:51:03.240 --> 00:51:04.980
I think perhaps one
more question for me,

00:51:04.980 --> 00:51:09.580
and then I'm keeping an eye
on the audience questions.

00:51:09.580 --> 00:51:11.850
So this is a challenging
time for, obviously,

00:51:11.850 --> 00:51:13.140
many industries.

00:51:13.140 --> 00:51:15.490
We've just been actually
working with a retailer that's

00:51:15.490 --> 00:51:18.930
seen 90% drop in their revenue.

00:51:18.930 --> 00:51:22.110
But they recognize the
importance of that API strategy

00:51:22.110 --> 00:51:27.120
as a defensive and
an offensive tactic.

00:51:27.120 --> 00:51:32.170
I often feel like there's this
sweet spot for an incumbent.

00:51:32.170 --> 00:51:34.220
It has appetites.

00:51:34.220 --> 00:51:37.760
If there's too much kind
of competitive pressure,

00:51:37.760 --> 00:51:39.360
they can default to safety.

00:51:39.360 --> 00:51:41.660
And if there's not enough,
this other end of the scale,

00:51:41.660 --> 00:51:44.498
they kind of [INAUDIBLE].

00:51:44.498 --> 00:51:46.915
Now, because of the situation
now, I guess a lot of things

00:51:46.915 --> 00:51:49.750
are being shaken up.

00:51:49.750 --> 00:51:53.685
What do you think will
be compelling messages

00:51:53.685 --> 00:51:55.060
that our audience
could take away

00:51:55.060 --> 00:51:59.280
around this situational
awareness that we currently

00:51:59.280 --> 00:52:01.030
have, this kind of
landscape that we have,

00:52:01.030 --> 00:52:04.570
where so many industries
have been disrupted?

00:52:04.570 --> 00:52:06.070
What do you think
could be the three

00:52:06.070 --> 00:52:10.030
things to call out that would
be compelling messages going

00:52:10.030 --> 00:52:11.692
forward?

00:52:11.692 --> 00:52:13.900
SIMON TORRANCE: I think one
of the most useful things

00:52:13.900 --> 00:52:15.460
to do at this time
of the crisis is

00:52:15.460 --> 00:52:21.430
to spend a little bit of
time zooming out, zooming out

00:52:21.430 --> 00:52:24.250
from all the pressures
of the core business

00:52:24.250 --> 00:52:27.850
and looking to the future,
because the crisis is just

00:52:27.850 --> 00:52:30.120
accelerating
digitalization, as we know,

00:52:30.120 --> 00:52:33.080
and certain sectors
more than others.

00:52:33.080 --> 00:52:35.000
And if you've got--

00:52:35.000 --> 00:52:39.610
one of my colleagues gave this
great suggestion the other day.

00:52:39.610 --> 00:52:45.280
He said, why don't you just
apply 0.1% of your employees

00:52:45.280 --> 00:52:47.120
to look at the future?

00:52:47.120 --> 00:52:50.630
That can be, like, 40 people.

00:52:50.630 --> 00:52:53.448
Can you afford 40 people--

00:52:53.448 --> 00:52:55.490
some of them could be on
furlough doing nothing--

00:52:55.490 --> 00:52:57.680
to look into the future?

00:52:57.680 --> 00:53:00.980
Because if we don't do
that, we're acting blind.

00:53:00.980 --> 00:53:03.080
It's like walking
forward without knowing

00:53:03.080 --> 00:53:05.030
what the future is like.

00:53:05.030 --> 00:53:08.390
And we need to ask the
difficult questions.

00:53:08.390 --> 00:53:10.790
How quickly is cash
going to disappear?

00:53:10.790 --> 00:53:12.540
If you're in financial
services or retail,

00:53:12.540 --> 00:53:15.470
that's a really
critical question.

00:53:15.470 --> 00:53:17.783
If you're a retailer--

00:53:17.783 --> 00:53:19.700
if you don't have an
online marketplace, which

00:53:19.700 --> 00:53:21.200
is a type of
platform, how are you

00:53:21.200 --> 00:53:23.300
going to compete with
others that have got that?

00:53:23.300 --> 00:53:25.258
I mean, if you did have
it in the COVID crisis,

00:53:25.258 --> 00:53:28.430
you'd be doing really
well, like those that do.

00:53:28.430 --> 00:53:32.330
So allocate-- it's not much.

00:53:32.330 --> 00:53:36.920
But allocate some people
to look into that future

00:53:36.920 --> 00:53:40.220
and translate it for
the leaders of today

00:53:40.220 --> 00:53:43.370
so they can really understand
it and internalize it.

00:53:43.370 --> 00:53:46.910
And everybody's got enough
resources to do that.

00:53:46.910 --> 00:53:50.630
That would be the one most
important thing to do.

00:53:50.630 --> 00:53:52.070
And understand the
business models

00:53:52.070 --> 00:53:55.100
that are likely to be most
effective in that world, which

00:53:55.100 --> 00:53:57.030
will be those that
connect people together,

00:53:57.030 --> 00:53:58.610
platform-based business models.

00:53:58.610 --> 00:54:00.620
And then try and
translate what that

00:54:00.620 --> 00:54:03.230
means to the core business.

00:54:03.230 --> 00:54:05.630
And look at those
different types of platform

00:54:05.630 --> 00:54:07.910
plays I described and
the different ways

00:54:07.910 --> 00:54:09.500
of executing them.

00:54:09.500 --> 00:54:13.830
And create a plan that can
apply those to our business.

00:54:13.830 --> 00:54:16.490
And finally, make it tangible
for the existing business

00:54:16.490 --> 00:54:17.450
owners.

00:54:17.450 --> 00:54:19.430
So if we do this,
like BP has done,

00:54:19.430 --> 00:54:22.910
we expect or we will plan
for this type of impact

00:54:22.910 --> 00:54:25.000
on the core business,
as well as keeping

00:54:25.000 --> 00:54:28.650
us fit for the future
in the, if you like,

00:54:28.650 --> 00:54:32.840
the invent the future
part of the business.

00:54:32.840 --> 00:54:33.840
OLIVER OGG: Fabulous.

00:54:33.840 --> 00:54:36.870
That resonates
with some reading I

00:54:36.870 --> 00:54:38.940
was doing around
the companies that

00:54:38.940 --> 00:54:41.970
struggled after the financial
crisis and those that actually

00:54:41.970 --> 00:54:44.405
did reasonably well
after the crisis.

00:54:44.405 --> 00:54:45.780
And it was
interesting to reflect

00:54:45.780 --> 00:54:50.460
that those that did kind of
broadside horizontal cost

00:54:50.460 --> 00:54:56.490
cutting and didn't invest in
these specific opportunities

00:54:56.490 --> 00:55:02.660
were three times less
successful than those that

00:55:02.660 --> 00:55:06.710
took an opportunity to
cut costs in some areas

00:55:06.710 --> 00:55:11.520
but also think about the future.

00:55:11.520 --> 00:55:13.260
And [INAUDIBLE].

00:55:13.260 --> 00:55:15.820
OK, so we're coming
up towards the end.

00:55:15.820 --> 00:55:23.440
So let's move to just
another slot for Q&amp;A. Here's

00:55:23.440 --> 00:55:23.940
a question.

00:55:23.940 --> 00:55:25.230
It's a bit of a classic.

00:55:25.230 --> 00:55:27.188
And I think we touched
on some of this already.

00:55:27.188 --> 00:55:29.520
But I mean, how do you
position a platform strategy

00:55:29.520 --> 00:55:33.600
in a traditional company
[INAUDIBLE] CEO and the board

00:55:33.600 --> 00:55:38.335
down are very conservative?

00:55:38.335 --> 00:55:40.710
SIMON TORRANCE: Well, you've
got to spend time with them.

00:55:40.710 --> 00:55:44.010
It's like any type of education.

00:55:44.010 --> 00:55:46.710
Make it tangible
to them and show

00:55:46.710 --> 00:55:51.330
how it can impact the figures or
the numbers that interest them.

00:55:51.330 --> 00:55:52.750
So again, I go
back to that chart

00:55:52.750 --> 00:55:54.870
with the insurance
company saying,

00:55:54.870 --> 00:55:58.980
your current growth plans
show this trajectory.

00:55:58.980 --> 00:56:02.470
Well, maybe, at the
moment, it's a negative.

00:56:02.470 --> 00:56:06.870
And here, if you did
these things here,

00:56:06.870 --> 00:56:09.000
we believe it can deliver this.

00:56:09.000 --> 00:56:11.290
And that's the type of
interesting discussion

00:56:11.290 --> 00:56:11.790
you'd have.

00:56:11.790 --> 00:56:14.670
If you just do it on theory
and ideas and concepts

00:56:14.670 --> 00:56:18.270
that they've never heard or
they're not familiar with,

00:56:18.270 --> 00:56:20.440
then it tends to go in
one ear and out the other.

00:56:20.440 --> 00:56:23.250
So we need to make it tangible
for those types of people

00:56:23.250 --> 00:56:26.700
and put it in language that
is motivating for them.

00:56:29.850 --> 00:56:31.710
OLIVER OGG: Thank you.

00:56:31.710 --> 00:56:37.530
Grace-- sorry, I'm just
scanning the questions here.

00:56:37.530 --> 00:56:39.720
Yeah, OK.

00:56:39.720 --> 00:56:42.927
I guess if I can--

00:56:42.927 --> 00:56:43.760
perhaps another one.

00:56:46.580 --> 00:56:50.430
What kind of
platform types do you

00:56:50.430 --> 00:56:55.500
think are resonating
with the C-suites

00:56:55.500 --> 00:56:57.600
that you're talking
to right now?

00:56:57.600 --> 00:57:01.230
I mean, you talked about, let's
say, markets and external--

00:57:01.230 --> 00:57:04.440
marketplaces and
external ventures.

00:57:04.440 --> 00:57:07.492
What do you think are
particularly compelling right

00:57:07.492 --> 00:57:08.160
now?

00:57:08.160 --> 00:57:09.670
SIMON TORRANCE: Yeah, so
it's a very good question.

00:57:09.670 --> 00:57:10.840
It depends on the sector.

00:57:10.840 --> 00:57:15.690
So if you take retail, if
you don't have a marketplace,

00:57:15.690 --> 00:57:18.810
online marketplace, you're
just not able to compete.

00:57:18.810 --> 00:57:20.250
When your customers
want something

00:57:20.250 --> 00:57:24.740
and then they can't
find it on your site,

00:57:24.740 --> 00:57:26.920
they default back to
Amazon, of course.

00:57:26.920 --> 00:57:30.120
So if you're in retail,
just being in the game

00:57:30.120 --> 00:57:32.050
means you need a marketplace.

00:57:32.050 --> 00:57:35.250
But I mean, that's
just to be in the game.

00:57:35.250 --> 00:57:39.960
What I think is relevant
for all sectors is to--

00:57:39.960 --> 00:57:42.180
often people think about
platforms as being,

00:57:42.180 --> 00:57:45.030
let's create the Uber
or Airbnb of our sector.

00:57:45.030 --> 00:57:48.660
That's really difficult
and most of those fail.

00:57:48.660 --> 00:57:51.370
The better starting
point is when you say,

00:57:51.370 --> 00:57:53.190
let's create a
digital solution that

00:57:53.190 --> 00:57:57.030
solves a really big
problem for our customers.

00:57:57.030 --> 00:58:00.250
And if we design that right,
it can be a point solution

00:58:00.250 --> 00:58:01.050
to start with.

00:58:01.050 --> 00:58:03.060
But like any
software business, it

00:58:03.060 --> 00:58:07.380
can evolve into more of a
platform, open platform,

00:58:07.380 --> 00:58:08.490
over time.

00:58:08.490 --> 00:58:11.940
But that tends to be
the way that the unicorn

00:58:11.940 --> 00:58:13.050
businesses started out.

00:58:13.050 --> 00:58:16.920
They solve a particular problem
first and then they expand.

00:58:16.920 --> 00:58:18.900
And I would recommend
for most companies

00:58:18.900 --> 00:58:22.560
and most sectors is create
a portfolio of those.

00:58:22.560 --> 00:58:24.270
That's what BP is
doing at the moment.

00:58:24.270 --> 00:58:26.620
They're not trying to
create the Uber of energy.

00:58:26.620 --> 00:58:27.840
Way too complicated.

00:58:27.840 --> 00:58:31.470
From very practical
digital solutions

00:58:31.470 --> 00:58:34.080
that are addressing
a big, big problem

00:58:34.080 --> 00:58:36.505
in the market that
can also drive value

00:58:36.505 --> 00:58:37.380
to the core business.

00:58:37.380 --> 00:58:40.620
And that would be my single
biggest recommendation.

00:58:40.620 --> 00:58:43.320
If you remember the types
of platforms I talked about,

00:58:43.320 --> 00:58:46.350
the bottom two are probably
the best to start with.

00:58:46.350 --> 00:58:48.000
If you're in financial
services, think

00:58:48.000 --> 00:58:49.140
about [INAUDIBLE] finance.

00:58:49.140 --> 00:58:51.570
If you're in retail, you
need a marketplace just

00:58:51.570 --> 00:58:52.860
to be in the game.

00:58:52.860 --> 00:58:55.020
But you need a combination
of those things.

00:58:55.020 --> 00:58:57.180
And you need a
separate organization

00:58:57.180 --> 00:59:00.082
in which to build them.

00:59:00.082 --> 00:59:01.290
OLIVER OGG: Thank you, Simon.

00:59:01.290 --> 00:59:04.320
And what I love about
that is just the message.

00:59:04.320 --> 00:59:07.410
McKinsey recently in an article
about digital transformation

00:59:07.410 --> 00:59:10.760
in the time of crisis talked
about the mandate to be bold.

00:59:10.760 --> 00:59:13.080
So I think that's a great
place to end today's session.

00:59:13.080 --> 00:59:16.450
Thank you for everyone
for the questions today.

00:59:16.450 --> 00:59:19.770
I hope you've found that session
both helpful, interesting,

00:59:19.770 --> 00:59:21.310
and useful.

00:59:21.310 --> 00:59:24.790
We would also love to hear
your feedback on this event.

00:59:24.790 --> 00:59:28.080
So please do send your
ideas and suggestions

00:59:28.080 --> 00:59:30.450
through the feedback link here.

00:59:30.450 --> 00:59:32.010
Do stay tuned.

00:59:32.010 --> 00:59:34.770
Next online meetup
will be in September.

00:59:34.770 --> 00:59:36.720
So please share your
thoughts so we can make

00:59:36.720 --> 00:59:38.980
the next edition even better.

00:59:38.980 --> 00:59:40.150
Thank you for your time.

00:59:40.150 --> 00:59:43.070
I look forward to
seeing you next time.

